{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_nontrivial_at_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_nontrivial_at_three","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/NakayamaGradingObstruction.lean#L81-L86","doc":"**`g_ρ` is a nontrivial ℤ/2 grading, so it is not the identity value `+1` everywhere.**\nConcretely `n([1,1,1]) = 3` is odd (for `λ = [1^3] ⊢ 3`), witnessing a degree where the\nrow grading is `−1 ≠ +1`. This is the smallest concrete obstruction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_odd","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/NakayamaGradingObstruction.lean#L77-L79","doc":"The row grading takes the value `−1` on odd degree (the bar-odd sector witness). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_ne_nakayama_of_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_ne_nakayama_of_odd","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/NakayamaGradingObstruction.lean#L68-L75","doc":"**The obstruction (odd sector).** On any partition with odd `n(λ) = k`, the row grading\n`g_ρ` takes the value `−1`, which differs from the symmetric-Nakayama value `+1`. Hence\n`g_ρ` is NOT the Nakayama automorphism of a symmetric algebra — this refutes Angle A on\nthe algebras (`R_n`, `ℂS_n`, `H_n(q)`) that carry the partition-indexed simples. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_eq_nakayama_iff_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign_eq_nakayama_iff_even","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/NakayamaGradingObstruction.lean#L54-L66","doc":"**The row grading matches the symmetric-Nakayama value ONLY on even degree.** Over any\n`CommRing`, `(−1)^k = 1 ↔ Even k`. So the sign carrier `g_ρ` agrees with a symmetric\nalgebra's Nakayama automorphism (`ν = id`, value `+1`) precisely on the bar-even\nsector. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.rowGradingSign","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/NakayamaGradingObstruction.lean#L50-L52","doc":"The ζ₄-evaluated principal **row** grading `g_ρ` on `V_λ`: `q^{2n(λ)}|_{q=i} = (−1)^{n(λ)}`\n(the Δ_λ sign carrier; see `PrincipalGradingJetOrder`). Parameterised by the degree `k = n(λ)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.nakayamaSymmetricValue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.NakayamaGradingObstruction.nakayamaSymmetricValue","kind":"def","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/NakayamaGradingObstruction.lean#L46-L48","doc":"The symmetric-algebra Nakayama value on a simple: a symmetric Frobenius algebra has\n`ν = id`, which acts as the constant `+1` on every simple `V_λ`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}